#sleela 1.10
// examples/extends/document_grouping.sleela
// The document-sheet meaning of `extends` (syntax 1.10): one .sleela document
// naturally points to other documents, organized by a GROUPER. The grouper is
// the organization term that says what structure the referenced documents form:
//
//   linear   -- linear reals: an ordered chain (this -> target -> ...),
//               bounded by the congruent-linear maximum (3024).
//   group    -- an unordered set gathered under one organizer,
//               bounded by the complexity-degree maximum (4 members).
//   services -- a group fronted by one Server-of-Services document the
//               members register under (grouped, then a server of services).
//
// `to` and the grouper words stay ordinary identifiers (not keywords), so they
// remain free to use elsewhere; recognition is anchored on `extends`.

extends to NextChapter linear;                       // linear reals
extends to { Intro, Body, Appendix } group;          // grouped set
extends to { Orders, Billing } services ClearingHub; // group + Server of Services

class ExtendsDocument {
    void main() {
        print("document extends to: 1 linear chain, 1 group, 1 services group");
    }
}
